77:    * Starts a Ratpack app, defined by the given closure.
78:    * <p>
79:    * This method is used in Ratpack scripts as the entry point.
80:    * <pre class="groovy-ratpack-dsl">
81:    * import ratpack.session.SessionModule
82:    * import static ratpack.groovy.Groovy.*
83:    *
84:    * ratpack {
85:    *   bindings {
86:    *     // example of registering a module
87:    *     add(new SessionModule())
88:    *   }
89:    *   handlers {
90:    *     // define the application handlers
91:    *     get("foo") {
92:    *       render "bar"
93:    *     }
94:    *   }
95:    * }
96:    * </pre>
97:    * <h3>Standalone Scripts</h3>
98:    * <p>
99:    * This method can be used by standalone scripts to start a Ratpack application.
100:    * That is, you could save the above content in a file named “{@code ratpack.groovy}” (the name is irrelevant),
101:    * then start the application by running `{@code groovy ratpack.groovy}` on the command line.
102:    * <h3>Full Applications</h3>
103:    * <p>
104:    * It's also possible to build Groovy Ratpack applications with a traditional class based entry point.
105:    * The {@link ratpack.groovy.GroovyRatpackMain} class provides such an entry point.
106:    * In such a mode, a script like above is still used to define the application, but the script is no longer the entry point.
107:    * Ratpack will manage the compilation and execution of the script internally.
108:    *
109:    * @param closure The definition closure, delegating to {@link ratpack.groovy.Groovy.Ratpack}
110:    */
111:   public static void ratpack(@DelegatesTo(value = Ratpack.class, strategy = Closure.DELEGATE_FIRST) Closure<?> closure) {
112:     try {
113:       RatpackScriptBacking.execute(closure);
114:     } catch (Exception e) {
115:       throw uncheck(e);
116:     }
117:   }
